AI-Driven Autonomous Defense for Banking Software Supply Chains: A Regulation-Aware, Adversarially Robust Framework with Self-Integrity, Certified Dual Robustness, and Continual Learning


ouadi Maakoul + Claude AI + Deepseek 

Abstract

Modern banking software supply chains are vast, dynamic, and exposed to sophisticated AI-augmented attacks, while regulators demand continuous third‑party oversight, real‑time explainability of automated decisions, and strict data sovereignty. This thesis presents RASCDS — a Regulation‑Aware Autonomous Supply Chain Defense System — that tightly integrates a multi‑agent behavioral detection engine, a neuro‑symbolic explainability layer with an independent symbolic verifier, a differentially private and Byzantine‑robust federated learning protocol, and a dual‑certified adversarial robustness framework. The architecture introduces several novel contributions: (i) a self‑integrity protocol that cryptographically attests every AI model artifact and monitors behavioral consistency, closing the recursive AI supply chain blind spot; (ii) joint deterministic certification against both evasion attacks (via AGNNCert) and training‑time poisoning attacks (via PGNNCert), a first in any security system; (iii) a two‑stage Logic Tensor Network training strategy with rule pruning that makes neuro‑symbolic explainability computationally tractable at banking‑scale graphs; (iv) the Jurisdiction‑Aware Federated Protocol with a distributed validation mechanism addressing the trust circularity in Byzantine‑resilient private aggregation; (v) formal verification of the multi‑agent orchestrator’s finite state machine for safety‑critical properties; and (vi) a comprehensive continual learning framework with drift detection, strategic forgetting, and differentially private elastic weight consolidation. The result is a rigorously specified system with formal proofs for certified dual robustness, compound false‑positive rate, DP‑Byzantine compatibility, symbolic explanation fidelity under an independent verifier, and regulatory ontology consistency.---

1. Introduction

1.1 The Banking Software Supply Chain Challenge

A single mission‑critical banking application integrates 300–500 open‑source components, 35–50 external APIs, and 8–15 major third‑party vendors across multiple cloud providers, exposing between 2,000 and 3,500 potential attack paths. Software supply chain attacks have increased by over 600% since 2022, and adversaries increasingly employ machine learning to craft novel, evasive threats at scale (ENISA, 2023; Sonatype, 2024). Banks now face adversaries who exploit AI to automate vulnerability discovery, generate polymorphic malware, and — most dangerously — insert malicious code through AI‑generated developer personas that accumulate trust over months before activating.

Simultaneously, the regulatory landscape has tightened. The Digital Operational Resilience Act (DORA, Regulation 2022/2554), the NIS2 Directive, the EU AI Act, and NYDFS Part 500 impose strict requirements for continuous third‑party risk monitoring, advanced threat detection, 24‑hour incident notification, and full explainability of automated security decisions. Most recently, the European Commission’s Digital Omnibus Package of November 2025 introduced a single entry point for cyber incident reporting across NIS2, DORA, GDPR, and the Cyber Resilience Act — harmonising reporting obligations but creating new tensions in classification: DORA Article 19 requires initial notification within 4 hours of classification, while NIS2 Article 23 allows 24 hours. A single incident may now simultaneously trigger both obligations, and a defense system must resolve these conflicts correctly and transparently.

Financial institutions find themselves in a narrowing window to close infrastructure gaps, particularly at the data provenance and audit evidence layer. Fewer than 50% of enterprises currently monitor more than half of their extended software supply chain, and runtime security controls consistently detect threats too late, underscoring the urgency of build‑time validation and behavioural monitoring.

1.2 The AI‑Governance Paradox

Traditional defenses — periodic vendor questionnaires, static software bills of materials, signature‑based detection — collapse under the weight of AI‑powered adversaries. Yet inserting deep learning into the security stack introduces the AI‑governance paradox: high‑performance models are opaque, making it impossible to produce the regulation‑grade audit trails required by DORA and the AI Act’s transparency mandates. Furthermore, the AI models themselves become new attack surfaces, vulnerable to adversarial evasion, data poisoning, model extraction, and — critically — to attacks on their own software supply chain. Over 100 large language models hosted on public repositories have been found to contain hidden backdoors capable of executing malicious code (Trend Micro, 2025). A defense system that does not secure its own AI supply chain is recursively fragile.

Compounding this paradox is a new threat vector: agentic AI coding assistants. Tools such as GitHub Copilot, Claude Code, and Cursor are now deployed in banking software development pipelines, but they are susceptible to prompt injection attacks — the PromptPwnd technique enables adversaries to inject malicious instructions through issue titles or pull request descriptions, leaking secrets and compromising CI/CD pipelines. The Lies‑in‑the‑Loop (LITL) attack defeats human‑in‑the‑loop confirmations by manipulating the AI agent to present fabricated safe context. These attacks operate before a commit is ever written, rendering commit‑time detection alone insufficient.

The central research question is:

How can an AI‑based autonomous defense system for banking software supply chains simultaneously (i) detect novel, AI‑generated attacks including agentic AI prompt injection with a false positive rate below 1%, (ii) provide real‑time, regulation‑mapped explanations for every alert that are independently verifiable, (iii) be provably robust against both evasion and poisoning adversarial attacks on its own AI components, and (iv) enable cross‑institutional threat intelligence sharing without violating data privacy or sovereignty?

1.3 Limitations of Current Approaches

Existing solutions fail on multiple dimensions. Commercial AI security tools operate as black boxes and rarely withstand targeted adversarial machine learning. Academic proposals for explainable intrusion detection lack integration with regulatory ontologies and do not scale to banking workloads. Federated learning for financial cyber threat intelligence remains nascent, with unresolved questions on the compatibility of differential privacy and Byzantine robustness in non‑IID settings. No existing system simultaneously addresses evasion‑time and poisoning‑time certified robustness for graph neural networks. Moreover, no existing system secures its own AI supply chain — the recursive blind spot that, if exploited, can collapse the entire defense apparatus.

Several practical barriers compound these theoretical limitations. The SLSA framework for software supply chain provenance, while the industry standard, faces significant adoption challenges: complex implementation and unclear communication across diverse ecosystems are the two main barriers identified in a large‑scale qualitative study of 1,523 SLSA‑related issues across 233 GitHub repositories. Mandating the highest provenance level as a hard gate would exclude most open‑source components from being monitored at all.

1.4 Contributions

This thesis presents RASCDS, a multi‑agent architecture that creates a live digital twin of a bank’s software supply chain, continuously assesses risk, and produces structured audit trails mapped directly to regulatory articles. The main contributions are:

1. A multi‑agent behavioral detection engine that fuses code semantics, API traffic, vendor risk signals, and the new maintainer behavioral graph for long‑horizon identity manipulation detection, integrated with a build‑time attestation gate using a progressive trust policy rather than a binary SLSA rejection threshold.
2. A neuro‑symbolic explainability layer grounded in a two‑stage Logic Tensor Network training strategy with rule pruning, making logical constraint learning tractable at banking‑scale graphs, with explanation fidelity measured against an independent human‑expert‑validated golden set.
3. A dual‑certified adversarial robustness framework that provides the first joint certification for evasion‑time robustness (AGNNCert) and training‑time poisoning robustness (PGNNCert), both giving deterministic 100% accurate guarantees under arbitrary joint perturbations of edges, nodes, and node features.
4. A federated learning protocol — BVDFed — that simultaneously achieves differential privacy and Byzantine resilience, augmented with a distributed validation protocol that eliminates the circular trust assumption on the aggregator’s validation set.
5. A Jurisdiction‑Aware Federated Protocol that classifies banks into regulatory tiers with graded information sharing, ensuring compliance with GDPR Article 46 and national banking secrecy laws.
6. A formal verification of the multi‑agent orchestrator’s finite state machine using the NuSMV model checker, proving safety, liveness, and deadlock‑freedom.
7. A continual learning framework integrating drift detection via the Kolmogorov–Smirnov test, strategic sample selection and forgetting, and differentially private elastic weight consolidation, with formal privacy budget accounting for the Fischer information computation.
8. A comprehensive experimental validation plan based on publicly available datasets (SynthChain, MalwareBench, npm malware datasets), a synthetic supply chain graph generator (G2A2), and a formal statistical power analysis of the independent symbolic verifier’s golden set.

The remainder of this document establishes the mathematical foundations, defines a comprehensive threat model with three new sub‑tiers, presents the system architecture with progressive trust and self‑integrity layers, provides detailed technical specifications with computational complexity characterisation, proves security guarantees including dual‑certified robustness and regulatory ontology consistency, and concludes with the validation plan.

