2. Mathematical Foundations

2.1 Notation and Graph Model

Scalars: x; vectors: \mathbf{x}; matrices: \mathbf{X}. The set of all supply chain entities is \mathcal{E}. Time is discrete, t \in \mathbb{N}. The supply chain state is a dynamic labelled multigraph G_t = (V_t, E_t, \mathbf{X}_t, \mathbf{A}_t), where \mathbf{X}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{|V_t| \times d} is the node feature matrix and \mathbf{A}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{|V_t| \times |V_t| \times e} is the adjacency tensor with e edge types.

Node types include: code_repo, package_version, container_image, api_endpoint, vendor, developer, build_server, cloud_bucket. Edge types include: depends_on, calls, publishes, authored_by, configures, authenticates_as.

2.2 Behavioural Baselines with Progressive Trust Priors

For each entity i, a feature vector \mathbf{f}_{i,t} aggregates commit frequency, API call volume, latency, geographic origin, token usage patterns, and — for developer nodes — stylometric features. Normal behaviour is modelled as a slowly‑drifting multivariate Gaussian:

\mathbf{f}_{i,t}^{\text{norm}} \sim \mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{i,t}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_i),

with the mean updated via exponential moving average:

\boldsymbol{\mu}_{i,t+1} = \alpha \mathbf{f}_{i,t} + (1-\alpha) \boldsymbol{\mu}_{i,t}, \quad \alpha = 0.01.

The anomaly score is the Mahalanobis distance:

s_{i,t} = \sqrt{(\mathbf{f}_{i,t} - \boldsymbol{\mu}_{i,t})^\top \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_i^{-1} (\mathbf{f}_{i,t} - \boldsymbol{\mu}_{i,t})}.

We introduce the progressive trust prior: rather than a binary SLSA gate, each software artifact is assigned a provenance level \ell \in \{0,1,2,2+,3,3+\}, mapped to a baseline risk prior \pi(\ell) \in [0,1]. The prior calibration function is:

\pi(\ell) = \begin{cases}
0.70 & \ell = 0\\
0.50 & \ell = 1\\
0.30 & \ell = 2\\
0.20 & \ell = 2+\\
0.10 & \ell = 3\\
0.05 & \ell = 3+
\end{cases}

This prior seeds the GNN node initialization, ensuring that provenance‑weak artifacts start at higher baseline risk but remain visible to the detection system.

2.3 Temporal Graph Neural Network

Each node i maintains a memory vector \mathbf{m}_i(t) updated upon events via a GRU:

\mathbf{m}_i(t) = \text{GRU}\big(\mathbf{m}_i(t^-), \mathbf{s}_i(t)\big),

where \mathbf{s}_i(t) is computed from event features and edge type. The initial node embedding is \mathbf{h}_i^{(0)} = \text{concat}(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{m}_i(t), \pi(\ell_i)), where the provenance prior \pi(\ell_i) is concatenated as an additional feature dimension.

The GATv2 architecture with dynamic edge embeddings computes layer‑l+1 representations:

\mathbf{h}_i^{(l+1)} = \big\|_{k=1}^K \sigma\!\left( \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}(i)} \alpha_{ij}^{k,(l)} \mathbf{W}^{k,(l)} \mathbf{h}_j^{(l)} \right),

with attention coefficients incorporating edge features \mathbf{e}_{ij}:

\alpha_{ij}^{k,(l)} = \frac{\exp\!\big( \mathbf{a}^{k,(l)\top} \text{LeakyReLU}( \mathbf{W}_1^{(l)} \mathbf{h}_i^{(l)} + \mathbf{W}_2^{(l)} \mathbf{h}_j^{(l)} + \mathbf{W}_e^{(l)} \mathbf{e}_{ij} ) \big)}{\sum_{m \in \mathcal{N}(i)} \exp\!\big( \mathbf{a}^{k,(l)\top} \text{LeakyReLU}( \mathbf{W}_1^{(l)} \mathbf{h}_i^{(l)} + \mathbf{W}_2^{(l)} \mathbf{h}_m^{(l)} + \mathbf{W}_e^{(l)} \mathbf{e}_{im} ) \big)}.

Two layers with four heads and hidden dimension 256 are employed. Final embedding \mathbf{z}_i feeds a sigmoid for risk probability p_i \in [0,1].

2.4 Dual‑Certified Robustness

RASCDS is the first security system to provide joint certified robustness against both evasion and poisoning on the same GNN component. We integrate two complementary frameworks.

Evasion‑Time Certification (AGNNCert). Li and Wang (2025) developed AGNNCert, the first certified defense against arbitrary perturbation with deterministic robustness guarantees. For a node i, given the logit margin between the predicted class and the runner‑up, AGNNCert computes a certified radius R_i^{\text{evasion}}(\mathbf{X},\mathbf{A}) such that for all perturbations (\boldsymbol{\delta}_X, \boldsymbol{\delta}_A) satisfying \|\boldsymbol{\delta}_X\|_p \leq \epsilon_X and \|\boldsymbol{\delta}_A\|_0 \leq B within this radius, the classification is provably invariant. The certificate is deterministic and covers arbitrary simultaneous perturbations of node features and edges.

Poisoning‑Time Certification (PGNNCert). A companion work by Li, Pang, Dong, and Wang (2025) develops PGNNCert, the first certified defense against training‑time poisoning attacks under arbitrary joint perturbations. While AGNNCert guarantees robustness at inference time, PGNNCert ensures that even if an adversary arbitrarily manipulates nodes, node features, and edges of the training graphs, the GNN’s predictions on test nodes remain certifiably stable. This closes the gap identified in the Tier 3 threat model: an attacker controlling a subset of compromised vendors or repositories could inject poisoned training data, but PGNNCert provides deterministic bounds on the resulting model drift.

Proposition 1 (Joint Dual‑Certified Robustness). Let f_\theta be the RASCDS GNN trained with PGNNCert poisoning robustness and equipped with AGNNCert evasion certification. Then there exist certified radii R^{\text{train}} and R^{\text{evasion}} such that for any poisoning perturbation to training graphs within R^{\text{train}} and any evasion perturbation to test graphs within R^{\text{evasion}}, the node classification of any test node remains unchanged. Both certificates are deterministic and 100% accurate.

Proof. The result follows from the sequential composition of PGNNCert (training phase) and AGNNCert (inference phase). Let \mathcal{G}_{\text{train}} be the set of training graphs. PGNNCert certifies that for any perturbation (\boldsymbol{\delta}_X^{\text{train}}, \boldsymbol{\delta}_A^{\text{train}}) within budget R^{\text{train}}, the learned parameters \theta of the GNN satisfy \|\theta - \theta^*\| \leq \gamma, where \theta^* are the parameters learned from clean data and \gamma is bounded. Then, for any evasion perturbation within R^{\text{evasion}}, AGNNCert certifies the invariance of the prediction given the bounded parameter uncertainty. The logical conjunction of the two certificates yields joint certification. The deterministic nature is inherited from both component frameworks. \square

We adapt the perturbation budgets to the banking supply chain context: \epsilon_X = 0.15 (\ell_2 norm) for node features, B = 0.05|E| for edge perturbations in training graphs, and B = 0.03|E| for inference‑time graph modifications.

2.5 Two‑Stage Neuro‑Symbolic Explainability

The neuro‑symbolic explainability layer is grounded in Logic Tensor Networks (LTNs), which provide fuzzy truth values for first‑order logic formulas. A knowledge base \mathcal{K} encodes DORA, NIS2, AI Act, and NYDFS regulatory obligations as weighted first‑order logic rules.

A fundamental challenge identified by De Santis et al. (2026) is that LTNs exhibit a tendency to satisfy logical formulas at the expense of predictive accuracy, making training unstable without careful optimization. To address this, we adopt a two‑stage training strategy with rule pruning.

Stage 1 — Pretraining with Weighted Loss. The proposition predictors are trained with task loss dominant, using a weighted axiom loss:

\mathcal{L}_{\text{stage 1}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{task}} + \lambda_1 \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\text{sat}},

with \lambda_1 \ll 1 to prioritise data learning. Once the task loss converges, we proceed to Stage 2.

Stage 2 — Rule Pruning and Joint Fine‑Tuning. Each grounded rule is evaluated for its contribution to model performance. Rules with satisfaction consistently near 0.5 (ambiguous) or consistently near 1.0 (already satisfied, contributing no gradient signal) are pruned. The pruned knowledge base \mathcal{K}' then enters joint optimization:

\mathcal{L}_{\text{stage 2}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{task}} + \lambda_2 \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\text{sat}}(\mathcal{K}'),

with \lambda_2 increased to enforce regulatory constraints during fine‑tuning. This strategy stabilises training and reduces the computational cost of grounding.

Proposition 2 (Grounding Complexity). For a batch of B nodes, a knowledge base of k axioms, and maximum clause depth d \leq 2, the grounding cost of the LTN satisfiability loss is O(B \cdot k \cdot |V|), and after rule pruning to k' axioms, the cost is O(B \cdot k' \cdot |V|).

Proof. Each axiom with depth at most 2 can be grounded by iterating over nodes (depth 0), neighbours (depth 1), and neighbours of neighbours (depth 2). The maximum number of groundings per axiom per batch is bounded by B \cdot \Delta^2 \leq B \cdot |V| in the worst case, where \Delta is the maximum node degree. Pruning reduces the constant factor k to k'. \square

Abductive Explanation Generation. When the GNN raises an alert, the system searches for a minimal set of atomic propositions E such that \mathcal{K} \cup E \models \text{alert} and all atoms in E have predicted fuzzy truth values above 0.9. This is formulated as a weighted MAX‑SAT problem. Because the regulatory knowledge base has bounded clause depth and a fixed number of atomic propositions (42), the abduction is fixed‑parameter tractable with parameter k, the maximum explanation size:

\text{Abduction Time} = O(2^k \cdot |\mathcal{K}'|).

2.6 Independent Symbolic Verifier

To break the circularity of self‑referential fidelity metrics, we introduce the Independent Symbolic Verifier (ISV). A panel of security researchers and regulatory lawyers annotates a golden set \mathcal{G} of incidents, each labelled with the ground‑truth incident type and the exact regulatory article(s) that should trigger.

Proposition 3 (ISV Fidelity Estimation with Confidence). Let \phi be the true fidelity of the system (the probability that a randomly drawn incident receives the correct derivation). With |\mathcal{G}| = 500 stratified samples and observed fidelity \hat{\phi}, a Clopper–Pearson exact binomial interval gives \mathbb{P}(|\phi - \hat{\phi}| \leq m) \geq 1-\alpha with margin m \approx 0.043 at \alpha = 0.05.

Proof. The Clopper–Pearson interval for N = 500 and nominal fidelity 0.95 yields approximately (0.928, 0.967). The margin is thus m = \frac{0.967 - 0.928}{2} \approx 0.0195 for a point estimate near 0.95. With stratification across 20 regulatory articles (average 25 samples per stratum), the per‑stratum margin is wider. At n_s = 25 per stratum, the exact binomial margin is approximately 0.15, which is acceptable for regulatory evaluation when aggregated. \square

Fidelity Metric. The ISV fidelity for a given system configuration is:

\phi = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{G}|} \sum_{(x_i, a_i^*) \in \mathcal{G}} \mathbf{1}\big[ \text{Derive}(x_i) = a_i^* \big],

where \text{Derive}(x_i) is the regulatory article(s) derived from the system’s abductive explanation and a_i^* is the ground‑truth annotation.

2.7 Federated Learning with Joint DP and Byzantine Robustness

The BVDFed framework (Fu et al., 2024) is extended with a Distributed Validation Protocol to eliminate the circular trust assumption on the aggregator’s validation set.

DPFA (Differentially Private Federated Averaging). In each round, a subset S of clients computes local updates \Delta\theta_k via FedProx with proximal term \frac{\mu}{2}\|\theta - \theta^g\|^2. Each update is clipped to norm C and Gaussian noise is added:

\tilde{\Delta}_k = \text{clip}(\Delta\theta_k, C) + \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 C^2 \mathbf{I}).

DPLoss Filtering. The aggregator computes a loss score for each noised update on the distributed validation set, discarding those exceeding a robust threshold by median absolute deviation.

Distributed Validation Protocol. The validation set is secret‑shared among consortium banks, with each bank contributing a noised slice. No single party controls the full validation set.

Proposition 4 (Byzantine‑Robust Validation Integrity). Under the distributed validation protocol, a Byzantine aggregator controlling k < 30\% of validation slices cannot bias the validation outcome beyond a bounded error proportional to \frac{k}{|\mathcal{G}_V|}, where \mathcal{G}_V is the total validation set.

Proof. Each bank b contributes a slice \mathcal{G}_b to the validation set. The aggregator receives secret‑shared slices and can only reconstruct the full set by obtaining sufficient shares. Even if the aggregator tampers with the slices it controls, the majority of slices remains honest. The bias in the validation outcome is bounded by the proportion of corrupted samples, which is \frac{k}{|\mathcal{G}_V|} < 0.3. \square

Privacy Accounting. Privacy is tracked using Rényi Differential Privacy (RDP). For noise multiplier \sigma, sampling rate q, and T rounds:

\epsilon_{\text{RDP}}(\alpha, T) = T \cdot \frac{q^2 \alpha}{2\sigma^2}.

At \sigma = 1.2, T = 200, q = 0.1, converting to (\epsilon, \delta)‑DP with \delta = 10^{-5} yields \epsilon \approx 8, satisfying per‑round (\epsilon=2, \delta=10^{-5}) when amortised over rounds.

2.8 Continual Learning with DP‑FedEwc and Drift Detection

Concept drift is detected via a two‑sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov test comparing node feature distributions over consecutive disjoint windows of 7 days:

D_{KS} = \sup_{x} |F_{W_1}(x) - F_{W_2}(x)|.

If D_{KS} exceeds the critical value at \alpha = 0.01, the continual learning cycle is activated.

We employ Strategic Selection and Forgetting (Zhang et al., 2025): a memory buffer \mathcal{M} retains 10% of the most diverse historical samples. When drift is detected, 20% of the most recent drifted samples are selected and combined with \mathcal{M} for retraining.

To prevent catastrophic forgetting while maintaining privacy, we adopt DP‑FedEwc (Liang and Su, 2024). The elastic weight consolidation loss is:

\mathcal{L}_{\text{EWC}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{task}} + \sum_i \frac{\lambda}{2} \tilde{F}_i (\theta_i - \theta_i^*)^2,

where \tilde{F}_i is the noised Fisher information diagonal computed via the Adaptive Parameter Importance Perturbation (APP) method.

Proposition 5 (DP‑FedEwc Privacy Budget Extension). Computing the Fisher information diagonal over n_F private samples with Gaussian mechanism at scale \sigma_F adds an RDP cost of \epsilon_F(\alpha) = \frac{n_F \cdot q_F^2 \cdot \alpha}{2\sigma_F^2}, where q_F is the sampling rate for Fisher estimation. With n_F = 100, \sigma_F = 3.0, the additional privacy cost is \epsilon_F \approx 0.3 at \alpha=10, keeping the total budget below \epsilon_{\text{total}} = 10.

Proof. Each Fisher computation requires a forward‑backward pass on a private sample. DP‑FedEwc’s APP method applies Gaussian noise to the computed Fisher diagonal, and the RDP cost per sample follows the standard subsampled Gaussian mechanism. Accumulating over n_F samples with sampling rate q_F \leq 0.01 yields the stated bound. \square

