3. Threat Model

3.1 Adversary Tiers

Tier 1 — Conventional Supply Chain Attacks. Dependency poisoning (malicious package uploads), CI/CD pipeline compromise (injecting backdoors at build time), vendor credential theft, and software update manipulation.

Tier 2a — AI‑Enhanced Attacks. Automated vulnerability discovery via deep reinforcement learning processing 10^4 packages per hour, generative deepfake social engineering targeting vendor employees with 40–60% credential theft success rates, and AI‑predicted dependency confusion where internal package names are inferred with high precision.

Tier 2b — Long‑Horizon Identity Manipulation (SockPuppet). An adversary deploys AI‑generated developer personas — “SockPuppets” — that contribute legitimate code for a period of 6–12 months to build reputation. These personas are equipped with coherent GitHub histories, Stack Overflow participation, and AI‑generated personal blogs. Once sufficient trust has been accumulated and commit rights have been granted to critical repositories, backdoors are injected. The attack exploits the 90‑day behavioural baseline window by staying within normal variance until the trust threshold is breached. Detection requires cross‑repository identity consistency checks and stylometric analysis over extended observation windows.

Tier 2c — Agentic AI Development Pipeline Attacks. Adversaries exploit AI coding assistants embedded in the software development lifecycle. The PromptPwnd class of vulnerabilities enables prompt injection through untrusted issue titles, pull request descriptions, or commit messages, causing AI agents to leak secrets, edit repository data, or execute privileged commands. At least five Fortune 500 companies are known to have been affected by PromptPwnd as of December 2025. The Lies‑in‑the‑Loop (LITL) attack defeats human‑in‑the‑loop safety confirmations by fabricating false safety context, enabling the AI agent to execute malicious commands with user approval. The s1ngularity attack demonstrated end‑to‑end prompt injection exploits compromising agentic systems across all major AI coding assistants. This tier operates before code reaches the commit stage, requiring pre‑commit monitoring of AI assistant session behaviour.

Tier 3 — Adversarial Machine Learning Against the Defense System. The attacker has white‑box or black‑box access to RASCDS’s AI components. Capabilities include:

· Evasion attacks on the GNN via simultaneous node‑feature and graph structure perturbation.
· Training‑time poisoning attacks manipulating the graphs used for GNN model updates, including attacks on the federated learning training data contributed by compromised participants.
· Model extraction and inversion via querying confidence scores.
· Adversarial misuse of explanations: using generated justifications to infer decision boundaries and craft optimal evasion patterns.
· RASCDS‑internal supply chain attacks: poisoning the CodeBERT model weights, inserting malicious configuration files into AI model repositories, or injecting backdoors through fine‑tuning adapter chains.

3.2 Defender Assumptions

· Cryptographic public‑key infrastructure is available for artifact signing.
· The federated aggregator operates inside an Intel SGX secure enclave.
· The proportion of malicious clients in any federation round is strictly less than 30%.
· Human security analysts are available to confirm Level 2 alerts.
· AI coding assistant session logs are available to the Code Integrity Agent via an enterprise‑deployed monitoring shim.

3.3 Security Requirements

1. SR1 — Detection under Evasion and Poisoning: ≥95% TPR for Tier 2a/b/c and Tier 3 attacks within perturbation budgets certified by AGNNCert and PGNNCert.
2. SR2 — System False Positive Rate: Compound alert FPR ≤ 1.0% under normal operating conditions, derived analytically from component FPRs under a logical‑AND gating mechanism.
3. SR3 — Explainability Fidelity: ≥95% agreement with the Independent Symbolic Verifier golden set across all regulatory articles.
4. SR4 — Dual‑Certified Robustness: Every node classification shall be certifiably stable under joint evasion perturbations (AGNNCert) and training‑time poisoning perturbations (PGNNCert).
5. SR5 — Privacy‑Preserving Federated Learning: BVDFed with Distributed Validation Protocol provides (\epsilon=2,\delta=10^{-5})‑DP per round and Byzantine resilience with ≤30% malicious clients.
6. SR6 — Latency: p99 detection‑to‑alert pipeline ≤ 500 ms for critical API threats, ≤ 5 s for full dependency graph analysis.
7. SR7 — Self‑Integrity: Every AI model artifact loaded into RASCDS must pass cryptographic attestation, behavioural consistency monitoring, and CONFIGSCAN‑based configuration file scanning.
8. SR8 — Orchestrator Safety: The multi‑agent orchestrator shall be formally verified to satisfy safety, liveness, and deadlock‑freedom properties.
9. SR9 — Regulatory Consistency: The LTN knowledge base shall be proven free of contradictory mandatory obligations; regulatory conflicts shall be resolved via a formal lexicographic priority rule.

