4. System Architecture

4.1 Overview

RASCDS is a distributed microservices architecture comprising five horizontal layers orchestrated by a Regulation‑Aware Orchestrator. The addition of the Pre‑Commit Agentic Monitor, the Self‑Integrity Monitor, and the Progressive Trust Gate differentiates this architecture from prior versions.

The architecture integrates the Orchestrator as a meta‑agent that consumes alerts, verifies safety‑critical preconditions, and triggers tiered automated containment while generating DORA‑aligned incident reports. The Orchestrator’s internal logic is formally modelled and verified at design time, and all state transitions are immutably logged for audit. The architecture also supports regulatory updates through the Regulatory Ontology Update Protocol, enabling the system to incorporate changes from the Digital Omnibus without requiring redeployment.

4.2 Digital Twin Engine

All entities are continuously ingested from connectors (Kafka streams from Git, CI/CD servers, API gateways, cloud logs, AI coding assistant session logs). The engine hosts the following specialised agents:

Code Integrity Agent. A fine‑tuned CodeBERT model with function‑call‑graph augmentation. It performs out‑of‑distribution detection via a benign Gaussian Mixture Model on [CLS] embeddings. Adversarial robustness is achieved through contrastive learning in the embedding space. A Progressive Trust Gate verifies in‑toto attestations and assigns each artifact a provenance level, mapping to a prior risk score \pi(\ell) that seeds the GNN, instead of imposing a binary accept/reject decision.

Pre‑Commit Agentic Monitor. This new component intercepts AI coding assistant session logs. A lightweight Transformer‑based classifier processes sequences of tool invocations, context‑injection patterns (issue titles, PR descriptions), and command execution requests. It detects pattern signatures associated with PromptPwnd (untrusted user‑controlled content injected into prompts), LITL (fabricated safety context), and other prompt‑injection attacks before harmful code reaches the commit stage. Detection is independent of the commit‑time Code Integrity Agent, providing a layered defence.

API Security Agent. A Temporal Convolutional Network with dilated causal convolutions processes API call sequences. A Variational Autoencoder combined with a WGAN‑GP discriminator provides anomaly scoring. A differentiable Neural Turing Machine per API consumer maintains long‑term state. The VAE and discriminator are adversarially re‑trained nightly using synthetic exfiltration traffic generated by a dedicated GAN.

Vendor and Persona Risk Agent. On top of the Bayesian hierarchical vendor compromise model, a Maintainer Behavioural Graph tracks each developer’s commit rhythm, stylometric fingerprint, response velocity, and co‑authorship network across a 12‑month rolling window. A logistic‑regression‑based trigger fires when a developer gains commit rights to three or more critical banking dependency repositories within a 30‑day window — the hallmark of a SockPuppet insertion.

Self‑Integrity Monitor. Before any AI model artifact is loaded, its cryptographic hash is verified against a signed manifest conforming to the Model Artifact Trust Standard (MATS). At runtime, the Maximum Mean Discrepancy between current inference output and a trusted reference distribution is continuously monitored. The CONFIGSCAN scanner inspects configuration files in all model repositories for malicious operations. Any deviation exceeding a calibrated threshold triggers an immediate lockdown.

4.3 Temporal GNN with Dual Certification

The Graph Constructor incrementally updates subgraphs upon event triggers, avoiding full recomputation. The TGN‑GATv2 model computes per‑node risk probabilities.

AGNNCert and PGNNCert are applied in a two‑phase certification pass. At inference time, AGNNCert deterministically verifies that each high‑risk node’s classification is stable under the allowed joint perturbation budget. During periodic retraining (nightly or upon drift detection), PGNNCert certifies that the training graph perturbations introduced by any adversary do not exceed the poisoning robustness bound.

An adaptive certification scheme reduces overhead: nodes flagged by the fast anomaly scoring pass (Mahalanobis distance above the 90th percentile) undergo full AGNNCert certification, while routine nodes rely on empirical robustness from adversarial training.

4.4 Neuro‑Symbolic Explainability Unit

The LTN knowledge base is trained using the two‑stage strategy described in Section 2.5. The 42 atomic proposition predictors are small MLPs on top of the shared TGN‑GATv2 embeddings. When the GNN raises an alert, the abductive reasoner extracts a minimal explanation, which is compiled into a natural‑language narrative and a structured JSON audit trail with regulation article citations.

The ISV golden set provides an independent fidelity measurement. The AI‑BOM conforms to the CycloneDX AIBOM schema, extended to include configuration file hashes, fine‑tuning adapter chains, inference environment attestation, and a training dataset lineage provenance chain.

Regulatory Conflict Resolution. The Regulatory Ontology Update Protocol maintains a conflict graph \mathcal{C} = (R, E_c). Nodes R represent regulatory obligations; directed edges E_c represent conflicts (same trigger, different required action) or subsumptions (one obligation implies another). The Digital Omnibus single entry point harmonises NIS2, DORA, GDPR, and CRA incident reporting, but DORA Article 19 mandates initial notification within 4 hours of classification while NIS2 Article 23 permits up to 24 hours for certain incident types.

Theorem 1 (Regulatory Consistency). Under the lexicographic priority rule — strictest timeline takes precedence — the bounded knowledge base \mathcal{K} with 42 atoms and the current regulatory corpus (DORA, NIS2, AI Act, NYDFS) is consistent: no two rules produce contradictory mandatory actions for the same input.

Proof. By exhaustive enumeration of rule pairs in \mathcal{K} with non‑disjoint triggers. For each pair (r_i, r_j) with overlapping trigger conditions, we verify that their mandatory action sets are either identical, one subsumes the other, or they differ only in non‑mandatory recommendations. The lexicographic priority resolves the subsumption case: when DORA and NIS2 both require notification but with different timelines, the stricter (DORA 4‑hour) applies. The bounded atom set and finite regulatory corpus make the enumeration tractable. \square

4.5 Federated Learning Layer

The Federation Agent runs BVDFed with the Distributed Validation Protocol and Jurisdiction‑Aware Federated Protocol.

Jurisdiction‑Aware Tiering. Banks are classified into regulatory tiers (EU‑DORA, US‑NYDFS, UK‑FCA, etc.). Tier A banks share full DP‑protected gradient updates within a single jurisdiction via secure aggregation. Tier B banks share only model predictions on synthetic data. Tier C banks participate solely in differential evaluation rounds. For cross‑tier collaboration, each bank trains a regulatory shadow model locally, and only aggregated, noised statistics are exchanged.

4.6 Continual Learning Manager

The Continual Learning Manager implements the KS drift detector, SSF strategic selection/forgetting, and DP‑FedEwc with the APP Fisher noise method. The METANOIA pseudo‑edge mechanism adds virtual edges between nodes with historically co‑occurring anomalies, improving detection of slow‑burn SockPuppet attacks. The memory buffer \mathcal{M} is curated to preserve samples from all known attack categories, preventing catastrophic forgetting when novel patterns emerge.

4.7 Formally Verified Orchestrator

The Orchestrator is a finite state machine with the following formal structure. States: Idle, AlertEval, ExplainGen, VerifyPrecond, AutoContain, HumanConfirm, ReportGen, MitigationDone, Error. Transitions are guarded by conditions on GNN confidence, ISV fidelity, financial impact, and regulatory deadline:

· AlertEval: An alert transitions from Idle to AlertEval; evaluate confidence p and fidelity \phi.
· AutoContain: From AlertEval, if p > 0.95 \land \phi > 0.98 \land \text{impact} < \tau_{\text{fin}}, transition to AutoContain and execute Level 1 actions.
· HumanConfirm: Otherwise, transition to HumanConfirm and await analyst response.
· ReportGen: From AutoContain or HumanConfirm, transition to ReportGen and pre‑populate the DORA incident report.

Theorem 2 (Orchestrator Safety). The Orchestrator FSM never reaches a state where a Level 1 automated containment action is executed without both GNN confidence exceeding 95% and symbolic fidelity exceeding 98%.

Proof. The transition to AutoContain is guarded by the condition p > 0.95 \land \phi > 0.98. No other transition reaches AutoContain. The NuSMV model checker verifies this invariant over all reachable states in the FSM, which has been encoded with 8 states and 12 transitions. The LTL specification \mathbf{G}(\text{AutoContain} \implies (p > 0.95 \land \phi > 0.98)) is proven valid. \square

Theorem 3 (Orchestrator Liveness). Every alert eventually reaches either AutoContain or HumanConfirm.

Proof. The FSM contains no cycles except the Idle self‑loop and the Error recovery path back to Idle. From AlertEval, all transition guards are complementary and exhaustive (p > 0.95 \land \phi > 0.98 \land \text{impact} < \tau versus its negation). The model checker confirms the LTL specification \mathbf{G}(\text{AlertEval} \implies \mathbf{F}(\text{AutoContain} \lor \text{HumanConfirm})). \square

Theorem 4 (Orchestrator Deadlock‑Freedom). The FSM contains no deadlock states.

Proof. Every state has at least one outgoing transition. The Error state transitions back to Idle on timeout or manual reset. NuSMV verification confirms that no state has all outgoing transitions disabled for all possible variable valuations. \square

