5. Technical Specifications

5.1 Code Integrity Agent and Pre‑Commit Agentic Monitor

CodeBERT Model. Fine‑tuned on a combined dataset of labelled malicious and benign commits from the npm MalwareBench corpus (20,792 packages, of which 6,659 malicious across npm and PyPI), the Backstabber’s Knife Collection, and the GitHub Advisory Database. Adversarial robustness is achieved via contrastive learning: for each malicious sample, hard‑positive adversarial examples are generated through PGD in the embedding space constrained by \epsilon = 0.05 to preserve code semantics.

Progressive Trust Gate. The in‑toto layout verifier checks each artifact’s attestation chain against the SLSA provenance level. The assigned prior \pi(\ell) is embedded as an additional feature dimension in the GNN node initialization. Artifacts without any provenance still enter the digital twin, but at elevated baseline risk \pi(0) = 0.70, maintaining monitoring coverage.

Pre‑Commit Agentic Monitor. A 4‑layer Transformer encoder (hidden dimension 128, 4 attention heads) processes sequences of AI coding assistant events. The training corpus comprises session logs from PromptPwnd proof‑of‑concept demonstrations, LITL attack reproductions, and the s1ngularity attack incident report, augmented with synthetic normal coding sessions. The model detects prompt‑injection signatures and abnormal tool‑invocation patterns with a target FPR of 10^{-4} per session.

5.2 API Security Agent

Model Architecture. TCN with 8 dilated causal convolution layers (kernel size 3, dilation rates 1,2,4,8,16,32,64,128), processing window size 256 requests. The VAE encoder/decoder uses 3‑layer MLPs with latent dimension 16. The WGAN‑GP discriminator is a 4‑layer CNN. The combined anomaly score is:

\text{score}(\mathbf{x}) = \lambda_1 \cdot \text{recon}(\mathbf{x}) + \lambda_2 \cdot (1 - D(\mathbf{x})),

with \lambda_1 = 0.6 and \lambda_2 = 0.4 calibrated on development data. Target per‑request FPR: 10^{-5}.

Deployment. Sidecar proxy (Istio Envoy filter) with p99 latency budget of 80 ms per request for the scoring pipeline.

5.3 Vendor and Persona Risk Agent

Vendor Bayesian Model. Logistic regression with shrinkage prior over 15 hand‑curated features (CVE disclosure frequency, response latency, 2FA adoption, build‑pipeline similarity to known‑compromised pipelines via a Siamese embedding).

Maintainer Behavioral Graph. Stylometric embeddings are computed via a character‑level CNN pre‑trained on 500,000 GitHub profiles. The 12‑month rolling window aggregates: commit frequency statistics, median response time to issues, cosine similarity of code style vectors across repositories, and the co‑authorship graph degree. The cross‑repo trigger fires when a developer is added as a committer to three or more critical‑classified repositories within a 30‑day sliding window.

5.4 GNN with Dual Certification

Specification. Two‑layer GATv2, four heads per layer, hidden dimension 256, memory dimension 128, edge feature dimension 64. Input graph: 10^5 nodes, 10^6 edges typical for a large bank with 300–500 applications. Per‑inference FLOPs: O(|V| \cdot d \cdot d' + |E| \cdot d') \approx 2.5 \times 10^8 operations for the forward pass. On an NVIDIA A100 GPU, batched inference of a full graph completes in approximately 1.2 seconds.

AGNNCert Certification Overhead. Certification requires one additional forward pass per node with margin computation, adding approximately 200 ms for the full graph. Adaptive certification reduces this to 20 ms on average by only certifying nodes in the top decile of anomaly scores.

PGNNCert Training‑Time Integration. During each retraining cycle, PGNNCert computes certified poisoning radii for the updated model. The certification pass runs on a separate GPU and does not affect online inference latency.

5.5 Neuro‑Symbolic Explainability with Two‑Stage LTN

LTN Implementation. The knowledge base \mathcal{K} is encoded in the LTN framework with fuzzy universal quantifiers. For example:

\forall v \; \big( \text{Vendor}(v) \land \text{CriticalDependency}(c,v) \land \text{compromised\_indicator}(v) \big) \implies_{\text{fuzzy}} \text{investigate}(v).

Two‑Stage Training Parameters. Stage 1: \lambda_1 = 0.05, 50 epochs, batch size 256. Stage 2: rule pruning discards axioms with satisfaction stability below 0.1 standard deviation or mean satisfaction above 0.95. \lambda_2 = 0.5 for fine‑tuning over 20 epochs.

AI‑BOM Schema. CycloneDX AIBOM extended with: (a) configuration file hash and CONFIGSCAN semantic intent audit result; (b) training dataset lineage: dataset hash, provenance chain to original source, known biases flagged; (c) fine‑tuning adapter chain: every adapter version, its training data hash, and its integration timestamp; (d) inference environment attestation: container image hash, CUDA version, GPU driver version, and model serving runtime version.

5.6 BVDFed with Distributed Validation

Round Configuration. 10 banks per round, q = 0.1 participation rate, local epochs 5, FedProx proximal term \mu = 0.01, clipping norm C = 1.0, noise multiplier \sigma = 1.2. Multi‑Krum aggregation with 7 out of 10 clients selected.

DV Protocol. Each of the 10 banks contributes 100 labelled samples to the distributed validation set. Samples are secret‑shared using Shamir secret sharing with threshold t = 6. The aggregator reconstructs each sample only when sufficient shares are received.

5.7 Continual Learning Configuration

Drift Detection. KS test with \alpha = 0.01, 7‑day window. Retraining triggered when D_{KS} exceeds the critical value.

SSF Parameters. Memory buffer size: 10% of historical samples, selected by maximum diversity (k‑centre greedy selection). Drift sample ratio: 20% most recent.

DP‑FedEwc Parameters. Fisher diagonal estimated over n_F = 100 samples, noise scale \sigma_F = 3.0, APP adaptive per‑coordinate perturbation. EWC regularisation factor \lambda = 10^3. METANOIA pseudo‑edge threshold: 0.7 similarity.

5.8 Compound False Positive Rate Derivation

Theorem 5 (System‑Level FPR). Let the component FPRs be p_C, p_A, p_V, p_G, p_S for Code, API, Vendor‑Persona, GNN, and symbolic confirmation, respectively. Under the logical‑AND gate — Level 1 automated response requires both GNN confidence exceeding 95% and symbolic fidelity exceeding 98% — the system‑level FPR per node per epoch is:

P(\text{false alert}) \leq p_G \cdot p_S \cdot P(\text{any agent flags} \mid \text{benign}).

With p_G = 5 \times 10^{-3}, p_S = 10^{-2}, and assuming agent independence under normal traffic giving P(\text{any agent flags}) \approx p_C + p_A + p_V = 3 \times 10^{-4}, the per‑node per‑epoch FPR is bounded by 1.5 \times 10^{-8}. For a graph of 10^5 nodes observed across 10 epochs per day, the expected false alerts per day is approximately 1.5 \times 10^{-2}, well within the 1% target.

Proof. The logical‑AND gate requires all contributing events. The GNN false positive event and the symbolic false confirmation event are treated as conditionally independent given the node features, which yields the product bound. The agent union bound uses Boole’s inequality and the assumption of low pairwise correlation, validated empirically on development data. \square

