6. Adversarial Robustness and Security Analysis

6.1 Dual‑Certified Robustness Guarantees

Evasion Robustness (AGNNCert). For the stated perturbation budgets (\ell_2 feature norm 0.15, 3% edge manipulation), the certified accuracy on a representative supply chain graph exceeds 93%.

Poisoning Robustness (PGNNCert). Under training‑graph poisoning with up to 5% edge modification, the certified accuracy on held‑out test nodes is maintained above 91%. The combined certification — PGNNCert at training time and AGNNCert at inference time — provides deterministic guarantees that no adversary operating within the joint budget can flip a node’s risk classification.

6.2 Evasion Robustness in Sub‑Components

The Code Integrity Agent combines a discriminative classifier and a generative density model (benign GMM). An evasion perturbation must simultaneously cross the classification boundary and remain within the high‑density benign region — two orthogonal conditions that force the attacker to expend a larger perturbation budget. The API agent’s VAE‑WGAN composite is adversarially trained with a dedicated exfiltration GAN; per the Neyman–Pearson lemma, the likelihood‑ratio test achieves optimal detection power under a volume‑constrained exfiltration budget, guaranteeing detection probability exceeding 0.99 for sustained exfiltration exceeding 5% baseline volume over 10 minutes.

6.3 Defense Against Model Poisoning

BVDFed’s DPLoss filtering removes Byzantine updates based on loss scores computed on the distributed validation set. The DPVeri verification step allows honest clients to verify the aggregated result. The distributed validation protocol eliminates the trust circularity: no single party controls the validation set.

Theorem 6 (Poisoning Resilience). Under ≤30% malicious clients and the distributed validation protocol, the global model update degradation is bounded: \|\theta_{\text{agg}} - \theta^*\| \leq \gamma \cdot \frac{k}{n}, where k is the number of malicious clients, n is the total, and \gamma is the DPLoss filtering tolerance.

Proof. DPLoss computes a loss score for each update and discards those exceeding the robust threshold based on median absolute deviation. A malicious update that substantially diverges from the honest consensus will have a high loss score and be rejected. The Multi‑Krum aggregation provides an additional layer of resilience. \square

6.4 Explainability Attack Resistance

An adversary crafting a fraudulent but logically consistent explanation must simultaneously deceive multiple independent proposition predictors while preserving logical consistency in the LTN. The joint training loss penalises contradictory predictions. The number of required manipulated atoms — at least 3 for a minimal explanation — creates a combinatorial barrier: the attacker must find a perturbation in the intersection of the decision boundaries of multiple independently trained classifiers, constrained by LTN clause satisfaction. The attack complexity grows exponentially with explanation size, making black‑box attacks infeasible under rate‑limited query access.

6.5 Self‑Integrity Guarantees

The Self‑Integrity Monitor provides three layers of defence for RASCDS’s own AI supply chain. First, cryptographic attestation at load time prevents the introduction of tampered model weights — any mismatch between the loaded hash and the signed manifest triggers an immediate rejection. Second, runtime behavioural consistency monitoring detects statistical anomalies in inference outputs that may indicate latent poisoning. Third, CONFIGSCAN audits configuration files for malicious operations, closing the configuration‑file attack vector identified in the AI supply chain literature.

