7. Empirical Validation Plan

7.1 Datasets

Given the acknowledged absence of experimental results in this proposal, we define a rigorous empirical validation strategy utilising the following datasets:

· SynthChain (2026): A near‑production testbed providing a multi‑source runtime dataset with chain‑level ground truth, covering seven representative supply‑chain exploit scenarios across PyPI, npm, and native C/C++ supply‑chain cases.
· MalwareBench (2024): A labelled dataset of 20,792 packages (6,659 malicious) from npm and PyPI, annotated with behaviour categories and evasion techniques, enabling systematic evaluation of the Code Integrity Agent.
· NPM Malware Benchmark (2026): 6,420 malicious and 7,288 benign packages annotated with 11 behaviour categories and 8 evasion techniques, with evaluations against 8 detection tools across 13 variants.
· G2A2 Graph Generator (2024): An automated graph generator producing dynamic bipartite graphs with ground‑truth anomalies and realistic propagation patterns, parameterised to match the banking supply chain’s node and edge type schemas.
· CERT Insider Threat Dataset: For API behavioural baseline calibration and exfiltration detection evaluation.

7.2 Evaluation Protocol

Phase 1 — Component Evaluation. Each agent (Code Integrity, Pre‑Commit Agentic Monitor, API Security, Vendor‑Persona Risk) is evaluated in isolation on its respective dataset. Metrics: TPR, FPR, AUC, and adversarial robustness under PGD‑crafted perturbations.

Phase 2 — Integrated System Evaluation. The full RASCDS pipeline is deployed on SynthChain scenarios. Ablation studies isolate the contribution of: (a) dual certification (AGNNCert + PGNNCert vs. none), (b) the progressive trust prior vs. binary SLSA gate vs. no provenance, (c) the Pre‑Commit Agentic Monitor vs. commit‑time detection only, (d) the two‑stage LTN training vs. single‑stage vs. post‑hoc SHAP explanations, (e) BVDFed with distributed validation vs. FedAvg without DP or Byzantine protection.

Phase 3 — Federated Evaluation. A simulated 10‑bank consortium with non‑IID data distributions evaluates privacy‑utility trade‑offs, convergence rates, and resilience to injected Byzantine clients.

Phase 4 — Human Factors Study. A controlled study with 15–20 SOC analysts drawn from a partner banking consortium evaluates explanation quality, trust calibration, and response efficiency on a subset of 200 SynthChain alerts.

7.3 Statistical Power Re‑assessment

The ISV golden set is stratified across 20 regulatory articles with a minimum of 25 incidents per stratum, for a total of 500 incidents. Per the Clopper–Pearson analysis in Proposition 3, this provides adequate power to estimate overall fidelity within a ±2% margin. For per‑stratum estimates, the margin widens, and the ISV will be expanded to 60 incidents per high‑priority DORA article in Phase 3.

