8. Discussion

The RASCDS framework resolves the AI‑governance paradox by integrating eleven distinct technical contributions into a unified architecture: dual‑certified robustness, two‑stage neuro‑symbolic explainability with an independent verifier, joint DP‑Byzantine federated learning, formal orchestrator verification, continual learning with DP‑FedEwc, a progressive trust policy for provenance, pre‑commit agentic AI monitoring, AI‑BOM configuration scanning, regulatory conflict resolution, the distributed validation protocol, and a comprehensive self‑integrity layer.

Limitations remain. The framework does not cover hardware supply chain attacks, which are orthogonal to the software supply chain focus. The compound FPR derivation assumes models calibratable to the stated component FPRs; real‑world calibration requires access to representative banking datasets. The Jurisdiction‑Aware Federated Protocol’s tier definitions depend on evolving legal interpretations of cross‑border data transfer, which may shift with future regulatory amendments. The ISV golden set, while adequate for overall fidelity estimation, requires expansion for fine‑grained per‑article guarantees. Future work should extend the framework to the emerging paradigm of fully autonomous AI software engineering agents, where new classes of multi‑agent prompt‑injection and collaborative poisoning attacks will emerge in the software lifecycle.

Notwithstanding these limitations, RASCDS provides the first complete theoretical blueprint for a regulation‑aware, adversarially robust, self‑securing autonomous defense system for banking software supply chains, with formal proofs for every claimed guarantee and a credible path to empirical validation.



