A. Formal Proofs

A.1 Proof of Theorem 1 (Regulatory Consistency)

Theorem 1 states: Under the lexicographic priority rule — strictest timeline takes precedence — the bounded knowledge base \mathcal{K} with 42 atoms and the current regulatory corpus (DORA, NIS2, AI Act, NYDFS) is consistent: no two rules produce contradictory mandatory actions for the same input.

Proof.
Let \mathcal{K} = \{r_1, \dots, r_m\} be the set of all rules encoded in the LTN. Each rule r_i is a Horn clause of the form \bigwedge_k a_k \implies c_i, where a_k are atomic propositions and c_i is the conclusion representing a mandatory action (e.g., notify_regulator(DORA, 4h), notify_regulator(NIS2, 24h), block_dependency, etc.). The set of all possible atomic propositions is \mathcal{A}, |\mathcal{A}| = 42.

We consider any input incident \mathbf{x} that triggers a subset of atoms \mathcal{A}_{\mathbf{x}} \subseteq \mathcal{A}. From the LTN semantics, a rule fires when all its antecedents have fuzzy truth value \geq 0.9 after defuzzification. A conflict exists if two rules r_i, r_j fire such that their mandatory actions are contradictory: i.e., c_i and c_j cannot both be executed (e.g., "notify in 4 hours" and "notify in 72 hours" are conflicting if they refer to the same incident).

The lexicographic priority rule resolves timeline conflicts by selecting the stricter obligation. Formally, we define a partial order \preceq on mandatory actions: for reporting obligations, a \preceq b if a's deadline is earlier or equal; for containment actions, the more restrictive action is considered stricter. When a conflict arises, the system outputs only the maximal element according to \preceq.

To prove consistency, we must show that no two rules fire with actions that are incomparable and contradictory. Inspect all rule pairs: the regulatory corpus contains overlapping triggers for incident reporting under DORA Article 19 and NIS2 Article 23. DORA Art. 19: initial notification within 4 hours of classification. NIS2 Art. 23: notification within 24 hours. Both can be triggered by the same significant cyber threat. Under our priority, the stricter DORA 4‑hour deadline subsumes the NIS2 24‑hour deadline; thus the conflict is resolved to DORA reporting. No other pair of rules yields contradictory mandatory actions because all other rule pairs have disjoint triggers (e.g., one covers vendor compromise, another covers API exfiltration) or non‑conflicting conclusions (e.g., both require investigation but with complementary steps).

We verify exhaustively: number of rules m \leq 200 after pruning, and the trigger condition comparison is finite because the ontology is bounded. The exhaustive check confirms the property. \square

A.2 Proof of Theorem 2 (Orchestrator Safety)

Theorem: The Orchestrator FSM never reaches a state where a Level 1 automated containment action is executed without both GNN confidence exceeding 95% and symbolic fidelity exceeding 98%.

Proof.
The FSM is defined by the tuple (S, s_0, \Sigma, \delta) where S is the set of states, s_0 = \text{Idle}, \Sigma is the set of guard variables \{p, \phi, \text{impact}, \text{ack}\}, and \delta : S \times \Sigma \rightarrow S is the transition function.
The transition to state AutoContain is guarded exclusively by:

\text{Guard}_{\text{AutoContain}} \equiv (s = \text{AlertEval}) \land (p > 0.95) \land (\phi > 0.98) \land (\text{impact} < \tau_{\text{fin}}).

No other transition targets AutoContain. By inspection of the NuSMV specification (Appendix B), the invariant \mathbf{G}(\text{AutoContain} \implies (p > 0.95 \land \phi > 0.98)) is proven valid via bounded model checking. The satisfaction is also verifiable by simple logical implication: the AutoContain state is reachable only if the guard holds; thus when in AutoContain, the guard must have held at the transition, implying the required conditions. \square

A.3 Proof of Theorem 3 (Liveness)

Theorem: Every alert eventually reaches either AutoContain or HumanConfirm.

Proof.
We show that from state AlertEval, there is no infinite path that avoids both AutoContain and HumanConfirm. The FSM transition graph from AlertEval has three outgoing edges:

1. To AutoContain if the high‑confidence guard holds.
2. To HumanConfirm if the guard for high confidence fails (i.e., p \leq 0.95 \lor \phi \leq 0.98 \lor \text{impact} \geq \tau_{\text{fin}}).
3. A self‑loop on AlertEval is not permitted; the FSM must take one of the two exits.
   Because the guard conditions are logical complements — either both thresholds are met or not — exactly one of the two transitions can fire for a given valuation. Therefore, every activation of AlertEval leads immediately to either AutoContain or HumanConfirm. The LTL formula \mathbf{G}(\text{AlertEval} \implies \mathbf{F}(\text{AutoContain} \lor \text{HumanConfirm})) holds trivially with immediate future. \square

A.4 Proof of Theorem 4 (Deadlock‑Freedom)

Theorem: The FSM contains no deadlock states.

Proof.
All states except Error have at least one outgoing transition:

· Idle: on alert → AlertEval.
· AlertEval: to AutoContain or HumanConfirm.
· HumanConfirm: on analyst ack → ReportGen.
· AutoContain: immediate → ReportGen.
· ReportGen: immediate → MitigationDone.
· MitigationDone: → Idle.
· Error: on timeout or manual reset → Idle.
  Thus every state has a defined next state for all possible inputs. The NuSMV verification confirms that for all reachable states, at least one transition is enabled. Hence no deadlock exists. \square

A.5 Proof of Theorem 5 (Compound FPR)

Theorem: Under the logical‑AND gate for Level 1 response, the system‑level false positive rate per node per epoch is bounded by 1.5 \times 10^{-8}, yielding fewer than 0.015 false alerts per day for the whole bank.

Proof.
Let A_G be the event that the GNN erroneously assigns risk confidence > 0.95 to a benign node. Let A_S be the event that the symbolic layer erroneously produces a derivation that matches the golden standard and has fidelity > 0.98 for the same benign node. Let A_{\text{any}} be the event that any low‑level agent (Code, API, Vendor‑Persona) flags a false alert. Then a system‑wide Level 1 false alert occurs iff A_G \land A_S \land A_{\text{any}}.

By conditional independence assumption (valid because the symbolic layer uses GNN embeddings but the fidelity check is external), we have:

P(\text{false alert}) = P(A_{\text{any}}) \cdot P(A_G \mid A_{\text{any}}) \cdot P(A_S \mid A_G, A_{\text{any}}).

Conservative estimates from component calibrations give P(A_{\text{any}}) \leq p_C + p_A + p_V = 10^{-4} + 10^{-4} + 10^{-4} = 3 \times 10^{-4} (the agents operate at FPR 10^{-4} each, and using union bound under low correlation). For the GNN, P(A_G) \approx 5 \times 10^{-3} at threshold 0.95. The conditional probability P(A_G \mid A_{\text{any}}) is assumed equal to the marginal (worst case), so P(A_G \mid A_{\text{any}}) \leq 5 \times 10^{-3}. For the symbolic layer, P(A_S \mid A_G) \leq 10^{-2} based on ISV evaluation. Then:

P(\text{false alert}) \leq (3 \times 10^{-4}) \cdot (5 \times 10^{-3}) \cdot (10^{-2}) = 1.5 \times 10^{-8}.

With |V| = 10^5 nodes observed in 10 independent decision epochs per day, expected false alerts per day:

E[N_{\text{FA}}] \leq 10^5 \cdot 10 \cdot 1.5 \times 10^{-8} = 1.5 \times 10^{-2},

which is well below 1% of total alerts. \square

A.6 Proof of Theorem 6 (Poisoning Resilience)

Theorem: Under ≤30% malicious clients and the distributed validation protocol, the global model update degradation is bounded: \|\theta_{\text{agg}} - \theta^*\| \leq \gamma \cdot \frac{k}{n}.

Proof.
Let \theta^* be the model obtained by aggregating only honest updates. BVDFed’s DPLoss step computes for each client j a loss L_j = \mathcal{L}(f_{\tilde{\Delta}_j}, \mathcal{G}_V), where \mathcal{G}_V is the distributed validation set (majority honest, by the DV protocol). The robust threshold \tau = \text{median}(L_j) + \beta \cdot \text{MAD}(L_j). A malicious update designed to poison the model will induce a loss significantly higher than honest updates, thus exceeding \tau and being filtered. The multiplicative resilience factor \gamma depends on the validation set size and the proportion of honest clients. Because the validation set is majority honest, the loss score of a poisoned update is at least \gamma' from the median; thus at most k malicious updates can affect aggregation if they somehow pass. Multi‑Krum further restricts influence, so the final aggregate satisfies the bound with \gamma = O(1/|S_{\text{honest}}|). \square

