D. Privacy Budget Accounting

D.1 Rényi DP Accounting for BVDFed

We adopt the RDP accountant for subsampled Gaussian mechanism. With noise multiplier \sigma = 1.2, sampling rate q = 0.1, number of rounds T = 200, the RDP at order \alpha is:

\epsilon_{\text{RDP}}(\alpha) = T \cdot \frac{q^2 \cdot \alpha}{2\sigma^2}.

At \alpha = 10, \epsilon_{\text{RDP}}(10) = 200 \cdot \frac{0.01 \cdot 10}{2 \cdot 1.44} \approx 6.94. Converting to (\epsilon,\delta)‑DP with \delta = 10^{-5} using the tight conversion via the moments accountant gives \epsilon \approx 8.1. Per‑round, the amortized cost is (\epsilon_{\text{round}} \approx 2, \delta) as required.

D.2 DP‑FedEwc Fisher Budget

Computing the diagonal Fisher on n_F = 100 samples with noise \sigma_F = 3.0, sampling rate q_F = 0.01 (once per retraining epoch), adds:

\epsilon_{\text{RDP},F}(\alpha) = 1 \cdot \frac{0.0001 \cdot \alpha}{2 \cdot 9} \approx 5.56 \times 10^{-6} \alpha.

At \alpha = 10, this is 5.56 \times 10^{-5}, negligible. Even over 200 retraining epochs, total added \epsilon < 0.02, keeping overall budget below 10.

