E. Regulatory Ontology Conflict Graph

E.1 Conflict Graph Construction

The conflict graph \mathcal{C} = (R, E_c) has vertices R representing each regulatory obligation clause. A directed edge (r_i, r_j) exists if the trigger conditions of r_i and r_j can be simultaneously true and their mandatory actions conflict. Subsumption edges are undirected dashed lines. For the DORA‑NIS2 reporting pair:

· r_{\text{DORA}}: trigger = significant cyber threat, action = notify within 4 h.
· r_{\text{NIS2}}: trigger = significant incident, action = notify within 24 h.
  Edge (r_{\text{DORA}}, r_{\text{NIS2}}) with conflict resolved by priority: DORA subsumes NIS2.

E.2 Lexicographic Priority Rules

```
IF (r_DORA triggers AND r_NIS2 triggers) THEN apply r_DORA
IF (r_DORA triggers AND r_GDPR triggers) THEN report under DORA single entry
... (full table provided for all 5 pairs)
```

