G. Computational Complexity Bounds

G.1 LTN Grounding Complexity

For a batch of B nodes, knowledge base size k, maximum clause depth d \leq 2, the grounding step enumerates all bindings of variables to entities within the subgraph. The cost is O(B \cdot k \cdot \Delta^d), where \Delta is the average node degree (\approx 20). In the worst case \Delta = |V|, but pruning of irrelevant nodes reduces effective \Delta. With typical values, grounding a batch of 256 nodes takes \ll 100 ms on a modern CPU.

G.2 AGNNCert and PGNNCert Complexity

AGNNCert requires one additional forward pass per node to compute the certified radius via Lipschitz bounds. Complexity: O(|V| \cdot (|E|/|V|) \cdot d'), roughly equal to inference. Adaptive certification reduces average cost. PGNNCert during training computes bounds on the influence of training perturbations; its complexity is O(N \cdot d) per training sample where N is the maximum number of modified edges, dominated by gradient backpropagation.

All bounds are well within the allocated inference latency budget of 5 s for full graph certification on A100 GPU.

